{"cuisine/ghanaian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/ghanaian-cuisine","n":"Ghanaian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Ghana","intro":[["Ghanaian cuisine is the cooking traditions of Ghana. It centers on starchy staple foods, accompanied by either a sauce or soup and a source of protein. The primary ingredients for the vast majority of soups and stews are tomatoes, hot peppers, onions, and some local species. As a result of these main ingredients, most Ghanaian jollof rice, soups, and stews appear red or orange."],["Ghanaian foods heavily rely on traditional food crops grown in Ghana, combined with crops introduced through colonial and globalized crops, gardens and cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ghanaian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghanaian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Eat with Afia","ref":"site/eatwithafia-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"eatwithafia.com","note":"Plant based Ghanaian cooking from Afia Amoako.","url":"https://eatwithafia.com","suggested":true},{"name":"Fafa Gilbert (Ndudu by Fafa)","ref":"site/fafagilbert-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"fafagilbert.com","note":"Ghanaian recipe posts from cook and writer Fafa Gilbert.","url":"https://fafagilbert.com/tag/ghana-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Eat with Afia: A Celebration of Plant-Based Ghanaian Cooking","ref":"cookbook/eat-with-afia-a-celebration-of-plant-based-ghanaian-cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Afia Amoako, 2026","note":"Plant based Ghanaian recipes and family food traditions.","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/761621/eat-with-afia-by-afia-amoako/","suggested":true},{"name":"Ghana to the World: Recipes and Stories That Look Forward While Honoring the Past","ref":"cookbook/ghana-to-the-world-recipes-and-stories-that-look-forward-while-honoring-the-past","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Eric Adjepong","note":"Ghanaian and West African recipes from a Ghanaian American chef, published by Clarkson Potter.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780593234778","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":4,"links":[["Akadi","dish/akadi"],["La Tante DC10 Restaurant","dish/la-tante-dc10-restaurant"],["Omo tuo","dish/omo-tuo"],["Pito (beer)","dish/pito-beer"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Millet","ingredient/millet"],["Sorghum","ingredient/sorghum"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"African cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4a/Ghanaian_Rice_and_Chicken.jpg/500px-Ghanaian_Rice_and_Chicken.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Michael Sean Gallagher","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghanaian_Rice_and_Chicken.jpg"}},"cuisine/odia-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/odia-cuisine","n":"Odia cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["East Indian cuisine","cuisine/east-india-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["The cuisine of Odisha is the cuisine of the Indian state of Odisha. Compared to other regional Indian cuisines, Odia cuisine uses less oil and is less spicy, while nonetheless remaining flavorful. Rice is the staple food of this region. Mustard oil is used in some dishes as the cooking medium, but ghee (made of cow's milk) is preferred in temples. Odia foods are traditionally served either on brass or bronze metal plates, banana leaves, or disposable plates made of sal leaves."],["Odia cooks, particularly from the Puri region, were much sought after due to their ability to cook food in accordance with the Hindu scriptures."],["Yoghurt is used in many Odia dishes. Many sweets of the region are based on chhena (cheese)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"odia-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odia_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Away in the Kitchen (Odia home cooking)","ref":"site/awayinthekitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"awayinthekitchen.com","note":"Blog section devoted to Odia home recipes.","url":"https://www.awayinthekitchen.com/odia-home-cooking","suggested":true},{"name":"Archana's Kitchen (Oriya)","ref":"site/archanaskitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"archanaskitchen.com","note":"Odia cuisine section of Archana Doshi's large Indian recipe site.","url":"https://archanaskitchen.com/cuisine/oriya","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Odia Byanjana","ref":"cookbook/odia-byanjana","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Debasish Patanaik","note":"Survey of Odisha's cooking traditions with recipes, published by Aleph.","url":"https://www.alephbookcompany.com/book/odia-byanjana/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Dahibara Aloodum","dish/dahibara-aloodum"],["Dalma (dish)","dish/dalma-dish"],["Red ant chutney","dish/red-ant-chutney"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Dahi (curd)","ingredient/dahi-curd"],["Dahi vada","ingredient/dahi-vada"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Vada","ingredient/vada"],["Oecophylla smaragdina","ingredient/oecophylla-smaragdina"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["East Indian cuisine","cuisine/east-india-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e4/Dahibara_Aloodum_-_%E0%AC%A6%E0%AC%B9%E0%AC%BF%E0%AC%AC%E0%AC%B0%E0%AC%BE_%E0%AC%86%E0%AC%B3%E0%AD%81%E0%AC%A6%E0%AC%AE%E0%AD%8D_-_Odisha.jpg/500px-Dahibara_Aloodum_-_%E0%AC%A6%E0%AC%B9%E0%AC%BF%E0%AC%AC%E0%AC%B0%E0%AC%BE_%E0%AC%86%E0%AC%B3%E0%AD%81%E0%AC%A6%E0%AC%AE%E0%AD%8D_-_Odisha.jpg","w":500,"h":504,"by":"Ashish Kumar Nayak","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dahibara_Aloodum_-_%E0%AC%A6%E0%AC%B9%E0%AC%BF%E0%AC%AC%E0%AC%B0%E0%AC%BE_%E0%AC%86%E0%AC%B3%E0%AD%81%E0%AC%A6%E0%AC%AE%E0%AD%8D_-_Odisha.jpg","of":"Dahibara Aloodum"}},"cuisine/macedonian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/macedonian-cuisine","n":"Macedonian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of North Macedonia","intro":[["Macedonian cuisine is the cuisine of North Macedonia. It is influenced by Ottoman and Balkan cuisines. The relatively warm climate of the country provides excellent growth conditions for a variety of vegetables, herbs and fruits. Macedonian cuisine is also noted for the diversity and quality of its dairy products, wines, and local alcoholic beverages, such as rakija. Tavče gravče and mastika are considered the national dish and drink of North Macedonia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"macedonian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macedonian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Macedonia: The Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/macedonia-the-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Katerina Nitsou","note":"Macedonian-Canadian author's recipes and stories from North Macedonia, published by Interlink.","url":"https://www.simonandschuster.ca/books/Macedonia-The-Cookbook/Katerina-Nitsou/9781623718794","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":6,"links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["İmam bayıldı","dish/imam-bay-ld"],["Makalo","dish/makalo"],["Pindjur","dish/pindjur"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"],["Tarator","dish/tarator"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Aubergine","ingredient/aubergine"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Juglans regia","ingredient/juglans-regia"],["Qatiq","ingredient/qatiq"],["Strained yogurt","ingredient/strained-yogurt"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/50/Christmas_Eve_%28Badnik%29_Dinner_Table_Macedonian.jpg/500px-Christmas_Eve_%28Badnik%29_Dinner_Table_Macedonian.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Crnorizec","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christmas_Eve_(Badnik)_Dinner_Table_Macedonian.jpg"}},"cuisine/buddhist-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/buddhist-cuisine","n":"Buddhist cuisine","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Religious cuisine or diet","intro":[["Buddhist cuisine is an Asian cuisine that is followed by monks and many believers from areas historically influenced by Mahayana Buddhism. It is vegetarian or vegan, and it is based on the Dharmic concept of ahimsa (non-violence). Vegetarianism is common in other Dharmic faiths such as Hinduism, Jainism and Sikhism, as well as East Asian religions, like Taoism. While monks, nuns and a minority of believers are vegetarian year-round, many believers follow the Buddhist vegetarian diet for celebrations."],["In Buddhism, cooking is often seen as a spiritual practice that produces the nourishment which the body needs to work hard and meditate. The origin of \"Buddhist food\" as a distinct sub-style of cuisine is tied to monasteries, where one member of the community would have the duty of being the head cook and supplying meals that paid respect to the strictures of Buddhist precepts. Temples that were open to visitors from the general public might also serve meals to them and a few temples effectively run functioning restaurants on the premises. In Japan, this culinary custom, recognized as shōjin ryōri or devotion cuisine, is commonly offered at numerous temples, notably in Kyoto. This centuries-old culinary tradition, primarily associated with religious contexts, is seldom encountered beyond places like temples, religious festivals, and funerals. A more recent version, more Chinese in style, is prepared by the Ōbaku school of zen, and known as fucha ryōri (普茶料理); this is served at the head temple of Manpuku-ji, as well as various subtemples. In modern times, commercial restaurants have also latched on to the style, catering both to practicing and non-practicing lay people."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buddhist_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":3,"rows":[{"name":"Korean Temple Cooking: Lessons on Life and Buddhism, with Recipes","ref":"cookbook/korean-temple-cooking-lessons-on-life-and-buddhism-with-recipes","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Hoo Nam Seelmann, 2025","note":"Profile of Buddhist nun and temple cook Jeongkwan Snim, with her vegan temple recipes.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781964786186","suggested":true},{"name":"Wookwan's Korean Temple Food: The Road to the Taste of Enlightenment","ref":"cookbook/wookwan-s-korean-temple-food-the-road-to-the-taste-of-enlightenment","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Wookwan, 2018","note":"English-language temple cookbook by Korean Buddhist nun Wookwan.","url":"","suggested":true},{"name":"The Enlightened Kitchen: Fresh Vegetable Dishes from the Temples of Japan","ref":"cookbook/the-enlightened-kitchen-fresh-vegetable-dishes-from-the-temples-of-japan","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Mari Fujii","note":"Kodansha book of Japanese Zen temple (shojin ryori) vegetable cooking.","url":"https://penguinrandomhouse.com/books/563547/the-enlightened-kitchen-by-mari-fujii","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Kajitsu","dish/kajitsu"],["Saikan","dish/saikan"]]},{"h":"Within Buddhist cooking","count":2,"links":[["Buddhist vegetarianism","cuisine/buddhist-vegetarianism"],["Korean temple cuisine","cuisine/korean-temple-cuisine"]]}],"cont":null,"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/2/2d/Chinese-buddhist-cuisine-taiwan-1.jpg","w":500,"h":378,"by":"Pratyeka at the English-language Wikipedia","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese-buddhist-cuisine-taiwan-1.jpg"}},"ingredient/mentha":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/mentha","n":"Mentha","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 6 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["Mentha, also known as mint, is a genus of flowering plants in the mint family, Lamiaceae. It is estimated that 18 to 24 species exist, but the exact distinction between species is unclear. Hybridization occurs naturally where some species' ranges overlap. Many hybrids and cultivars are known."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-mentha"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mentha"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",[["Akuri","dish/akuri"],["Shirazi salad","dish/shirazi-salad"]]],["Arab cuisine","cuisine/arab-cuisine",[["Shawarma","dish/shawarma"]]],["Azerbaijani cuisine","cuisine/azerbaijani-cuisine",[["Dovga","dish/dovga"]]],["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Chakapuli","dish/chakapuli"]]],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Akuri","dish/akuri"]]],["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",[["Yayla çorbası","dish/yayla-corbas"]]],["Uzbek cuisine","cuisine/uzbek-cuisine",[["Dovga","dish/dovga"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Spinach","ingredient/spinach"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yogurt","ingredient/yogurt"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Scrambled egg","ingredient/scrambled-egg"],["Scrambled eggs","ingredient/scrambled-eggs"],["Anethum","ingredient/anethum"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Petroselinum","ingredient/petroselinum"],["Plum","ingredient/plum"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4d/Mentha_spicata-IMG_6186.jpg/500px-Mentha_spicata-IMG_6186.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"C T Johansson","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mentha_spicata-IMG_6186.jpg"}},"dish/apio-appetizer":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/apio-appetizer","n":"Apio (appetizer)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Sephardic Jewish cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Apio, also known as apiu, is a Balkan Jewish appetizer made from celeriac and carrot and served cold. It is customary to serve the appetizer on the three pilgrimage festivals before the ritual meals."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-apio-appetizer"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apio_(appetizer)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Sephardic Jewish cuisine","cuisine/sephardic-jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":9,"links":[["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Carrots","ingredient/carrots"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Celery root","ingredient/celery-root"],["Kosher salt","ingredient/kosher-salt"],["Lemon juice","ingredient/lemon-juice"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"]]},{"h":"More from Sephardic Jewish cuisine","links":[["Bulemas","dish/bulemas"],["Fritas de prasa","dish/fritas-de-prasa"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/84/Apiu.jpg/500px-Apiu.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"w:he:משתמש:assayas","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apiu.jpg"}},"ingredient/kosher-salt":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/kosher-salt","n":"Kosher salt","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Kosher salt or kitchen salt (also called cooking salt, rock salt, kashering salt, or koshering salt) is coarse edible salt usually without common additives such as iodine, typically used in cooking and not at the table. It consists mainly of sodium chloride and may include anticaking agents."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-kosher-salt"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosher_salt"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Sephardic Jewish cuisine","cuisine/sephardic-jewish-cuisine",[["Apio (appetizer)","dish/apio-appetizer"],["Fritas de prasa","dish/fritas-de-prasa"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Carrots","ingredient/carrots"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Celery root","ingredient/celery-root"],["Lemon juice","ingredient/lemon-juice"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Leeks","ingredient/leeks"],["Matzo meal","ingredient/matzo-meal"],["Potatoes","ingredient/potatoes"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/07/Comparison_of_Table_Salt_with_Kitchen_Salt.png/500px-Comparison_of_Table_Salt_with_Kitchen_Salt.png","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Lexlex","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comparison_of_Table_Salt_with_Kitchen_Salt.png"}},"ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils","n":"Animal fats and oils","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 21 cuisines","intro":[["Animal fats are lipids derived from animals which are used by the animal for a multitude of functions, or can be used by humans for dietary, sanitary, and cosmetic purposes. Depending on the temperature of the fat, it can change between a solid state and a liquid (oil) state. Chemically, both fats and oils are composed of triglycerides. Although many animal parts and secretions may yield oil, in commercial practice, oil is extracted primarily from rendered tissue fats from livestock animals like pigs, chickens and cows. Dairy products yield animal fat and oil products such as butter."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-animal-fats-and-oils"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal_fat"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":21,"groups":[["Afghan cuisine","cuisine/afghan-cuisine",[["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]],["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways",[["Akutaq","dish/akutaq"]]],["Armenian cuisine","cuisine/armenian-cuisine",[["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]],["Athabascan foodways","cuisine/athabascan-foodways",[["Akutaq","dish/akutaq"]]],["Azerbaijani cuisine","cuisine/azerbaijani-cuisine",[["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]],["Bashkir cuisine","cuisine/bashkir-cuisine",[["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]],["Bengali cuisine","cuisine/bengali-cuisine",[["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]],["Crimean Tatar cuisine","cuisine/crimean-tatar-cuisine",[["Chebureki","dish/chebureki"]]],["Iñupiaq foodways","cuisine/inupiaq-foodways",[["Akutaq","dish/akutaq"]]],["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",[["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]],["Kazakh cuisine","cuisine/kazakh-cuisine",[["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]],["Kyrgyz cuisine","cuisine/kyrgyz-cuisine",[["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]],["Lakota foodways","cuisine/lakota-foodways",[["Pemmican","dish/pemmican"]]],["Métis foodways","cuisine/metis-foodways",[["Pemmican","dish/pemmican"]]],["Tajik cuisine","cuisine/tajik-cuisine",[["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"]]],["Tatar cuisine","cuisine/tatar-