ifera is an oilseed commonly known as turnip rape, field mustard, bird's rape, and keblock."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-brassica-rapa"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brassica_rapa"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Pot-au-feu","dish/pot-au-feu"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Pot-au-feu","dish/pot-au-feu"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d9/Brassica_rapa_plant.jpg/500px-Brassica_rapa_plant.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"TeunSpaans","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brassica_rapa_plant.jpg"}},"ingredient/frikadelle":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/frikadelle","n":"Frikadelle","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A Frikadelle is a rounded, flat-bottomed, pan-fried meatball of ground meat, often likened to the German version of meatballs. The origin of the dish is unknown. The term Frikadelle is German, but the dish is associated with German, Nordic, and Eastern European cuisines. They are one of the most popular meals in Poland, where they are known as kotlety mielone (literally \"ground cutlets\") or regionally sznycle (\"schnitzels\")."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-frikadelle"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frikadelle"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian-American cuisine","cuisine/italian-american-cuisine",[["Spaghetti and meatballs","dish/spaghetti-and-meatballs"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Meatball","ingredient/meatball"],["Spaghetti","ingredient/spaghetti"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["To